#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157367 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a94de121-a4bc-5c49-b381-af42e7f0741f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a94de121-a4bc-5c49-b381-af42e7f0741f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547689} :answer-field/id #uuid "72a14dfe-4aac-5b96-99e1-2adb2bdf2921" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4bf276d-b40b-5d47-b840-e4ace9d966fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-126763" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{″}(0).$\n\n${f}^{″}(0)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}+\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\  & =2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{2xy}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\cdot 2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}+2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4{x}^{2}y+2\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the point $(0,0)$ lies on $f,$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,0)} & =-4\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot 0+2\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8ccf237-4b9b-58f9-b076-7fd0f6ca2fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8ccf237-4b9b-58f9-b076-7fd0f6ca2fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.02" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547800} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfb2ed2d-7ad9-5182-a69d-d31e503dd0c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9b186f94-2456-5e8b-8019-128c4ccb5b71" :question/math-academy-id "q-126764" :question/problem "Use Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(0.2)$ using two steps of equal size.\n\n$y\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(0.2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=0.2$ is $0.2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0.2-0}{2}=0.1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=0.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}=2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 0.1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+0.1=0.1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0.1$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}=2\\cdot 0.1\\cdot \\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}=0.2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0.2\\cdot 0.1=0.02 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0.1+0.1=0.2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0.02=0.02\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0.1$ | $0$ | $0.2$ | $0.02$ |\n| $0.2$ | $0.02$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $f(0.2)≈0.02.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100894 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d692ba23-e86a-5e24-a39c-8c7caf6f455f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d692ba23-e86a-5e24-a39c-8c7caf6f455f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547772} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d2156ef-39e9-59d1-b08e-81876504abac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bc80b6f-e02e-58f0-97b4-8ea651af23ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-126765" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation. Express your answer in the form $y=y(x).$\n\n$y\\,\\,=\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C),\\,\\,$where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y & ={x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a perfectly valid general solution. However, if we want to write $y=y(x)$ explicitly, then we can solve for $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+C)\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99112850-043d-53e5-8d61-fecb0317bd11" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by Separating the Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9f82827-bfc3-518b-84b7-a88995159d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(x^2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9f82827-bfc3-518b-84b7-a88995159d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(x^2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547690} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf767100-f800-58a2-a235-bc232ccd9496" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11ed723f-8fe2-5c56-8bb0-841acdfb0940" :question/math-academy-id "q-126766" :question/problem "Given that $|C|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ use the general solution to the equation to find the function $f(x).$\n\n$f(x)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The general solution is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+C).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({0}^{2}+C) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(C) \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of ${y}^{′}=xy$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdd566f7-15e2-59d9-bf6a-1fa3598399b9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maclaurin Expansion of a Composite Function Using a Standard Maclaurin Expansion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4894c5e3-d189-5188-b2cd-8e0fcb9cb0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4894c5e3-d189-5188-b2cd-8e0fcb9cb0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547477} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6d8bb1-dc72-57b4-8ae4-3352e7114253" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547886} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a023b1-849e-5a22-8537-3b1e243bc4d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{10}}{120}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19a023b1-849e-5a22-8537-3b1e243bc4d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{10}}{120}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547368} :answer-field/id #uuid "e53bb430-337d-5efc-a511-030700cf693c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547959} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "310709fe-e9c3-552c-943c-e064e286df88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x^6}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "310709fe-e9c3-552c-943c-e064e286df88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x^6}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547417} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef950b23-df29-5428-9d9a-cb0f2b045309" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1d89a0f-00b0-5608-ac35-e4c5c811a70d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126767" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $f(x).$\n\n$f(x)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,⋯\\,.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with ${x}^{2}$ in the above expansion and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}) & =({x}^{2})-\\frac{1}{6}({x}^{2})^{3}+\\frac{1}{120}({x}^{2})^{5}+⋯ \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{120}{x}^{10}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f133583-cb11-5007-bdef-641838aff5e4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Definite Integrals Using Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c6372b2-3f3a-5d2d-9b9b-d69b71a08c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{42}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c6372b2-3f3a-5d2d-9b9b-d69b71a08c40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{42}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547404} :answer-field/id #uuid "96f0fd95-c9b0-5cbe-abcc-ca8956c4e2ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3b460f4-8f03-59ea-8d4e-3d88c9d8d99f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126768" :question/problem "Using the first two non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $f(x),$ approximate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ Write your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\,≈\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have that\n\n$$\nf(x)≈{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ by integrating the above series term-by-term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ≈{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{42}{x}^{6})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{42}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{42}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72ee4476-9ccd-5e3a-a1aa-efe576b4d55e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence of a Given Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0ee0055-9260-50e8-82d0-59ef2f03f218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0ee0055-9260-50e8-82d0-59ef2f03f218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547734} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee13cf7b-506a-556c-9c39-4af06c9d75a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8679792d-1ee6-50ba-bf59-17896dc979de" :question/math-academy-id "q-126769" :question/problem "Given that the Maclaurin expansion of $f(x)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!},\n$$\n\ncalculate the radius of convergence $R$ of this power series.\n\n$R\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the radius of convergence, we apply the ratio test.\n\nSo, let's denote ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{4n+6}}{(2n+3)!}\\cdot \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(2n+2)(2n+3)}| \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{1}{(2n+2)(2n+3)}| \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ebe73f6-f39b-5a49-b3c3-de18743301b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x^5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ebe73f6-f39b-5a49-b3c3-de18743301b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x^5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547523} :answer-field/id #uuid "185d4856-04c2-50f7-84b9-410c7305e5d2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547851} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c973ac4-5ab5-5a93-bb56-ca24aab38187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^9}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c973ac4-5ab5-5a93-bb56-ca24aab38187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^9}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547449} :answer-field/id #uuid "d014467f-0ee5-505a-9570-429795048b5c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547949} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2b7960-3515-5fdb-851d-2acdacca5d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e2b7960-3515-5fdb-851d-2acdacca5d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547495} :answer-field/id #uuid "d246d886-a271-5eac-b996-a935f3cc4523" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f6c3d65-e721-5449-9ddc-958a81a54048" :question/math-academy-id "q-126770" :question/problem "Calculate the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)\\,\\,=\\,\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,⋯\\,.$" :question/worked-solution "We have that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{4n+2}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nWriting the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{120}{x}^{10}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}^{′}(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x-{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{9}+⋯\\,.\n$$"}]}
